# Line endings are decided here, not by whoever's machine last wrote a file.
#
# This project is edited from Windows (\\wsl.localhost\...) and runs entirely in
# Linux containers. Without this file a shell script written from the Windows
# side reaches the container with CRLF endings and bash refuses it outright —
# `/bin/bash^M: bad interpreter` — and `scripts/dev.sh` simply will not run.
# Nothing in the error points at line endings, so it costs an hour to find.
#
# eol=lf means git stores LF and checks out LF on every platform. Shell scripts
# are the ones that FAIL; everything else is listed because a file that flips
# endings shows up as a whole-file diff, which buries the three lines that
# actually changed and makes review worthless.
#
# Carried over from the reference project, which learned it the hard way.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf

*.py        text eol=lf
*.ts        text eol=lf
*.tsx       text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf
*.jsx       text eol=lf
*.mjs       text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.css       text eol=lf
*.html      text eol=lf
*.sql       text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.cfg       text eol=lf
*.ini       text eol=lf
*.env*      text eol=lf
.env*       text eol=lf
Dockerfile* text eol=lf
*.conf      text eol=lf
Makefile    text eol=lf

# Binary — never normalise, never diff as text.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.webp      binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
*.ttf       binary
*.otf       binary
*.eot       binary
*.zip       binary
*.gz        binary
*.tar       binary
*.dump      binary
*.sqlite3   binary
